[Music]  hi everyone my name is Joey and welcome  to the first of a few guides where I'll  be guiding you through some of the  basics of Dragon Ball fighters now you  might feel at home with the game if you  played a little bit of Marvel vs. Capcom  3 but if you're new to fighting games  the mechanics can be a little bit  overwhelming so what I'm gonna be doing  is breaking them down for you bit by bit  now there's a lot to get through so  let's get started  so we're going to start off with the  basic mechanics that all characters have  and the first one is the Auto combo now  you can debate all you want about  whether or not high level players should  use it but they're actually quite useful  now mashing light attack will get you a  basic combo and there won't be a super  at the end it'll be just a small amount  of damage but it also gives you a random  Dragon Ball and that's mechanic we'll  discuss later and go more in depth in  the future video and now mashing medium  attack will get you a slightly stronger  combo ending with a super now it does  give you more damage but if you want to  save meter or if you're looking to fill  up your Dragon Balls the light attack  will be your go to auto combo next up is  the super dash and if you played any of  the beta you'll likely know what this is  if you have your default controls on  pressing r2 or our trigger will cause  your character to homed in on the  opponent starting a combo if it connects  and it's a fast and easy way to get in  and apply pressure it's safe on block  and can go through some projectiles but  if you rely too heavily on it it can get  countered by a simple anti-air attack or  a super so use it if you know that  they're not gonna react to it or if you  have advantage okay and then we have the  ki blast pressing the special button  will shoot some variation of a  projectile at least with most characters  you know it will differ from fighter to  fighter and you can mash it for extra  hits now these things won't do a ton of  damage and will most likely be eaten by  other stronger projectiles but it can be  good to use on the opponent's wakeup if  you input a quarter circle forward and  then special it will give you a bigger  attack typically some kind of comical ha  or an upgraded version of that  character's projectile now these things  are usually pretty good combo Enders so  for example if you do a light medium  hard air combo you can end it with  quarter circle Ford special and then  maybe go into a super after that and  while we're talking about ki there's  also the ki charge and this is done by  pressing light and special attack and  what you'll do is you'll stand in place  charging up your meter and it gets  faster as it goes up but unfortunately  you can't just  in the opponent's face you'll be pretty  much completely open to attack so use it  wisely or cover it with an assist  and speaking of assists every character  has one and while I'm not going to go  through all of them right now we don't  really have the time I'll just touch on  them briefly now every fighter has their  own unique assist and the trick is using  them in the appropriate moments if you  like rushing people down for example  Vegeta's downward key blastocysts could  be pretty useful or if you like  extending combos you can use kidd booze  or young physicists because those work  wonders  the dragon rush is your Universal throat  and so you'll dash for it a little bit  and then grab them and if you connect  with it it'll launch them up into the  air for a follow-up combo now this is  pretty good for catching people  off-guard or for people who like  turtling a little bit too much it's  pretty quick so try mixing it up with in  your rush contacts so maybe we can do a  block string of light medium hard  special and then maybe follow it up with  the dragon rush but it can be countered  with another dragon rush or another  normal attack and so if you do happen to  land dragon rush what you can also do  instead of combo is that you can follow  it up with a snapback now I'm not sure  what they're called in the game but snap  acts are Marv vs. Capcom term and so  here you what you can do is hold one of  your assistants during the dragon rush  and at the end of the throw it'll kick  out their point character and bring in  one of their remaining fighters you  really want to use this if one of their  reserve characters has a lot of blue  health as it'll get rid of all of it  when they come in so here's another  mechanic that you might be familiar with  if you play marvel and that's the  sparking Black's pressing light medium  hard and special tech all at the same  time will cause you to explode making  your character stronger and allowing you  to recover your blue health  automatically sounds a little bit like  x-factor again in this game it is a  comeback mechanic so it gets more  powerful the smaller your team is so  using it with your last character will  give them a huge boost in damage and  healing if your opponent is pelting you  with projectiles and you've had enough  of it  the Flex can be your friend holding back  and pressing special attack will cause  your character to well deflect anything  that sent their way and that includes  projectiles normal attacks super dashes  and yes even supers you can deflect an  entire super if you time it right and  the timing is only important on the  first hit so for any attacks that have a  follow-up  you know if someone does a bunch of key  blasts you can deflect them all just by  mashing the button  and finally the last one that I'll talk  about is vanish pressing medium and  heart attack will teleport your fighter  behind the opponent kicking them in the  back and also consuming one bar of meter  now you can use this to get out of  harm's way but it's actually more useful  in the middle of a combo as it'll extend  that combo by bouncing them off the wall  now vanish is essentially the gateway to  Dragonball fighters more advanced combos  you know it opens up so many  possibilities and you'll start thinking  about when you want to vanish and what  special moves and assists you want to  combine with it to cook up some nasty  mix ups so those were the basic  mechanics of Dragon Ball fighters I hope  this guide was helpful to all of you  I'll have another guide coming soon that  will go over some of the more advanced  mechanics so look out for that and  remember to visit gamespot.com for even  more dragon ball fighters news Easter  eggs and our review of the game as well  alright guys my name is Joey and thanks  for watching  we're in the shoots  [Music]

